<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n13789" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">dĭcātĭo</span></b> ,
			ōnis, f.
		1. dico, <i>no.</i> II. B. 1.. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>A formal declaration of intention to become a
				citizen</i>, <i><span class="en">Cic. Balb. 11</span></i> (v. the passage in
			its connection).—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b> = praedicatio, <i>a praising</i>.
			</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>A.</b> Prop.,
					<i><span class="en">Cod. Theod. 9, 3, 2</span></i>.
			—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>B.</b> As a title: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">tua dicatio,</span>”
				<i>your Reverence</i>,
				 <i><span class="en">Cod. Theod. 11, 30, 1</span></i>
			; Lact. Mort. pers. 48 al.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
